{:edb/db-before-t 1116 :edb/db-after-t 1117 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534429 50 #inst "2026-10-11T20:39:37.944Z" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186150167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150168 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186150167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150172 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186150167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421262 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186150167 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421277 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186150167 1078 #edb/ref 17592186150169 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186151905 1074 "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](images/63/63e7eee4a168f339b9dfb1fb69ca67a3747d567020ade08c2bbab3121b530b72.svg)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186151905 1074 "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n![](images/8c/8c9f75d71e362ec7207e00f67adecae7193e35972e46a783faa5aa1ea6c00301.png)\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =2x{({x}^{2})}^{′}-{x}^{2}{(2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186151906 1074 "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n![](images/19/19eae90f8f511056865f967ce8bf784261bd0f1b20cec65f478919e4b1c00078.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186151906 1074 "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n![](images/57/573b2182d03d7e15927368117c3e610cc018dbcda0711754083a2d8a3c95320d.svg)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2-(2x)\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+1)-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\left\\{ y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}$ is linearly independent on $\\mathbb R.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421312 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421313 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152278 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421309 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421310 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421311 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152305 1050 780.0 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152610 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_{1}, y_{2})$ for $y_{1}(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_{2}(x) = e^{-2x}$." 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186152610 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186152610 1074 "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{2x}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{e}^{-2x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-2{e}^{-2x})-{e}^{-2x}(2{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{0}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186153328 1064 "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nand it has the following important properties:\n\n> If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n> If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot deduce any information from $W = 0$ in cases where $y_1$ and $y_2$ are not known to be solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation." 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186153328 1064 "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n![](images/57/57c395afa6eecd7289b91a25e61b5310447cc3702daf019b0046615242f34057.svg)\nand it has the following important properties:\n\nIf $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\nIf $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot deduce any information from $W = 0$ in cases where $y_1$ and $y_2$ are not known to be solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation." 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186153329 1064 "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{1}^{'}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{'}-{({e}^{2x})}^{'}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions." 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186153329 1064 "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{2x})}^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions." 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186153330 1064 "Suppose $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$ is a set of functions defined on an interval $I\\subseteq R.$\n\n- The set is called **linearly dependent** on $I$ if there exist real numbers $k_1, k_2, \\ldots, k_n,$ *not all zero*, such that\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ \n\nIntuitively, at least one of the functions can be written as a linear combination of the others.\n\n- The set is called **linearly independent** on $I$ if the *only* way the linear combination\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x)\n$$\n\ncan equal $0$ for all $x \\in I$ is by taking\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions closely parallel the notions of linear dependence and independence for vectors in a vector space.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set of functions \n\n$$\n\\{ \\sin x, \\, \\cos x, \\, 2\\sin x \\}\n$$\n\ndefined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\sin x +\n0 \\cdot \\cos x +\n(-1) \\cdot 2\\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since not all the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly dependent.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186153330 1064 "Suppose $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$ is a set of functions defined on an interval $I\\subseteq R.$\n\n- The set is called **linearly dependent** on $I$ if there exist real numbers $k_1, k_2, \\ldots, k_n,$ *not all zero*, such that\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$  \n\nIntuitively, at least one of the functions can be written as a linear combination of the others.\n\n- The set is called **linearly independent** on $I$ if the *only* way the linear combination\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x)\n$$\n\ncan equal $0$ for all $x \\in I$ is by taking\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions closely parallel the notions of linear dependence and independence for vectors in a vector space.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set of functions \n\n$$\n\\{ \\sin x, \\, \\cos x, \\, 2\\sin x \\}\n$$\n\ndefined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\sin x +\n0 \\cdot \\cos x +\n(-1) \\cdot 2\\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since not all the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly dependent.\n\nLet's see another example." 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186307992 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305321 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186307992 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305321 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186307992 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306501 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186308124 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307251 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186308124 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306652 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186308124 1078 #edb/ref 17592186307251 13194139534429 17592186045417 false] [17592186308562 1071 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186308562 1071 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \no