 17592186045417 true] [17592186421306 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421276 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421306 1079 #uuid "e75553da-6c04-50a4-a94c-aab86bb8cabd" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421258 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421261 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421272 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421273 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421284 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1077 "selection" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421273 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421307 1079 #uuid "f206bfdf-3b37-567e-9742-b62602b37fa9" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421308 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421266 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421308 1075 #edb/ref 17592186421270 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421308 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421308 1077 "field-3" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421308 1078 #edb/ref 17592186421270 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421308 1079 #uuid "f3a2b6ff-740b-5c73-94e2-69f51c63499e" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421295 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421309 1069 "q-129803" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421309 1071 "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421309 1072 #uuid "16b925fc-3e3d-5f10-942e-39dcf0cbf5aa" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421309 1074 "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({e}^{x}){(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421297 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421299 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421308 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421310 1069 "q-334119" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421310 1071 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=-3x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421310 1072 #uuid "3b78d46d-4b29-5944-9afd-75dc247769fb" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421310 1074 "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3x & x+1 \\\\ (-3x)^{′} & (x+1)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3x & x+1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3x)(1)-(x+1)(-3) \\\\  & =-3x+3(x+1) \\\\  & =-3x+3x+3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421307 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421311 1069 "q-334252" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421311 1071 "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421311 1072 #uuid "46d6b627-57fc-5c4f-9c8a-dc31a7241e7a" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421311 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(0)-x(1)+x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2)-x(2x)+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421298 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421300 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421301 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421305 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421306 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1069 "q-334011" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1071 "Given the set of functions ${\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nFor all $x\\in R,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1072 #uuid "47c9e11d-3763-56a1-a6fd-21b1255f236b" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421312 1074 "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421294 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421296 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421302 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421303 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421304 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1069 "q-334026" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1071 "Given the set of functions ${{e}^{x},\\,x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nSuppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{k}_{2}\\cdot x=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${k}_{1}$ and ${k}_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=0,$ we get  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get  \n{{field-2}}.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is {{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1072 #uuid "c75ba2f7-e873-5513-857a-f8ee74ebb547" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true] [17592186421313 1074 "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}{e}^{x}+{k}_{2}x=0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(0)=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\n- If $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=0\\,⇒\\,2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=0.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{independent}.$" 13194139534429 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-06c7fbe1-9c5b-5cb0-b4a6-9f060759a858" 17592186421245 "answer-10ba94b5-d5f6-561b-b2e2-a78ef02bf1f4" 17592186421246 "answer-178c87ca-598d-5627-943a-1799fc01e9f7" 17592186421247 "answer-1886b4c5-85d1-54e5-8510-86fe8c320098" 17592186421248 "answer-1918dcc5-9c9c-5d4c-8fb6-13ae3046b611" 17592186421249 "